<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lutetia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lutetia was the Gallo-Roman town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> that grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the chief town of the Gallic Parisii. Caesar held an assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> there in 53 BCE, and in 52 BCE it was burned during the fighting against his legate Labienus. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it was rebuilt mainly on the Left Bank around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montagne-sainte-genevieve">Montagne Sainte-Geneviève</a>, with a forum, baths and an amphitheatre, and by the 4th century it was called Paris.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lutetia.jpg" alt="Lutetia" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Traumrune · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musee-Cluny-frigidarium_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Traces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> habitation, dating to about 4500 BCE, have been found along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> at Bercy and near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, and a larger settlement was established around the middle of the third century BCE by the Parisii, a Gallic tribe. The site was an important crossing of the Seine, where land and water trade routes met. Historians have long placed the Parisii's main settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, as Caesar's description of an island fortress suggests, but a large Gallic settlement found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanterre">Nanterre</a> between 1994 and 2005 has led some to doubt this. Defenders of the island note that the Parisii burned their oppidum and that the island has been rebuilt many times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Parisii first agreed to submit to Caesar, but in 52 BCE they joined the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>. Their forces were led by his lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulogenus">Camulogenus</a>, who burned the oppidum and the bridges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a> marched south to Melun, crossed the river there, returned towards the city and decisively defeated them. Traces of Roman occupation appear at the end of the first century BCE, under Augustus. The city was laid out on the left bank from the heights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montagne-sainte-genevieve">Montagne Sainte-Geneviève</a> down to the island, with a forum, baths, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and an amphitheatre that could hold 17,000 spectators. It was not the provincial capital, which was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>, but it grew through its position on trade routes.</p><p>In the middle of the third century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and Alemanni invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the unfortified city was threatened. Parts of the left bank, including the baths and amphitheatre, were abandoned and their stones used for ramparts round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, and the city shrank from a hundred hectares to a few small enclaves. According to legend Saint Denis brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> about 250. In 357 and 358 Julian moved the Roman capital of Gaul from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and his troops proclaimed him emperor in Lutetia in 360. Lutetia had gradually become Paris, the city of the Parisii, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> later made it his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Lutetia's name probably comes from a Celtic root meaning swamp or marsh. The Thermes de Cluny, public baths of the late first or early second century, are the largest and best-preserved Roman remains and now form part of the Musée de Cluny. The amphitheatre was discovered in 1867-68 during the building of Rue Monge, and a coalition including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> saved it from becoming a bus depot. Vestiges of the later civic basilica and baths can be seen in the archaeological crypt before Notre-Dame.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lutetia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A significant number of oppida became Roman cities, among them Vesontio (Besançon), Durocortorum (Reims), <b>Lutetia</b> (Paris) and Avaricum (Bourges).</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar mentions that he held a council of Gaul at <b>Lutetia</b> in 53 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii">Parisii</a></li></ul></section>
